  ('cs_creighton-abrams_career', 'ent_creighton-abrams', 'career', 'Career', 'Creighton Williams Abrams Jr. (15 September 1914 – 4 September 1974) was a United States Army general who commanded military operations in the Vietnam War from 1968 to 1972. He was then Chief of Staff of the United States Army from 1972 until his death in 1974.
In 1980, the United States Army named its then new main battle tank, the M1 Abrams, after him. The IG Farben building in West Germany was also named after Abrams from 1975 to 1995.

